Change history:

1.0.11 - craigde - Fix setting the number of pods (reported by terminal3).
         1.0.10's error reporting pinned it down: the "dishwasher-set-pods"
         appliance#control command now gets "400 ... malformed syntax - Invalid
         JSON" back for every operation. That command was reverse-engineered
         from the phone app in 2022 and GE has retired it - nothing in
         gehomesdk or Home Assistant's ge_home integration calls the control
         endpoint at all any more.
         What Home Assistant does instead, and what works, is write pods ERD
         0x301f directly: ge_home exposes pods as a writable GeErdNumber on
         that ERD, and gehomesdk encodes it through ErdIntConverter at its
         default 2-byte width. adjustNumberOfPods now does the same.
         Because an ERD write is absolute, "add" and "subtract" are computed
         here from the current podsRemaining reading instead of by the cloud,
         and now ask for a Refresh if that reading has not arrived yet. "set"
         is unaffected.
1.0.10 - craigde - adjustNumberOfPods diagnostics and argument hardening,
         after a report that setting the pod count stopped working while the
         SmartHQ phone app can still set it.
         Not a fix for the reported regression, and not claimed as one. The
         message this command sends is still byte-for-byte what the reference
         python SDK sends, and when invoked from the device detail page its
         payload was already well formed. What was wrong is that the command
         was fire-and-forget: nothing on the Hubitat side ever looked at the
         cloud's answer, so a rejection was completely invisible and there was
         no way to tell a changed API from a bad payload. The app now reports
         those rejections (see smartHQ_app 1.0.10) at warn level, so the log
         will name the reason the cloud gave without enabling debug logging.
         Also fixed here, real but a different path: Hubitat does not pass a
         NUMBER command parameter as one consistent type. The device detail
         page gives a BigDecimal, which serializes correctly as 5, but a Rule
         Machine custom action gives a String, which serialized as "5" - a JSON
         string where the API wants the integer 5 - and a non-numeric argument
         threw IllegalArgumentException out of the "number < 0" guard. Both
         arguments are now normalized, and invalid input is logged with a
         reason instead of silently doing nothing.
1.0.7  - craigde - Added rinse-aid level attribute (rinseAgentStatus:
         good/low/unknown) on ERD 0x3003. Byte-code mapping is model-specific
         and empirically derived (thanks jmp / terminal3 on the Hubitat forum).
